# TODO - Native ARM64 package and release mechanics

## Objective

Provide one ARM64 Mac application archive, one Safari-extension archive, Linux
development-package validation, and a release workflow that only publishes
already-validated Mac artifacts.

## Acceptance

The supported Mac product target is ARM64 only. General CI validates TypeScript,
roadmap integrity, the portable suite, and the Linux x64 development package.
Only after that job passes does the macOS ARM job build and verify the ARM64
app,
convert the shared WebExtension with Apple's Safari packager, compile the Xcode
project without signing, embed that containing app inside Blooket API.app,
recreate the final Mac archive, and exercise the packaged workspace through
Safari WebDriver.

A quarterly release tag invokes the reusable CI workflow. Release does not
compile, test, package, or reverify. After CI succeeds it checks the explicit
release flag and quarterly CalVer tag, downloads the exact CI artifact by name,
requires darwin-arm64.zip and publishes only that asset with empty notes for
later manual editing. Its Blooket API.app already contains the Safari companion.

Linux remains a development/validation target and is never a release asset.
There is no macOS x64 target in package assembly, updater selection, CI, or
Release.

## Implementation notes

The distribution assembler pins Node 24.21.0 and the admitted cloudflared
archive, embeds the runtime, workspace, canonical CLI, browser extension, native
dependencies, metadata, and icons, and creates the ARM64 app archive. The Linux
x64 package remains available for local and CI portability checks.

The reusable CI workflow is deliberately opt-in for a ci-* tag and also
available through workflow_call. Its macos-arm job depends on general
validation, requires an ARM64 runner, asserts the app archive before Safari
work, converts the already-built WebExtension, builds the generated Xcode
project, embeds Blooket API Safari.app plus the activation helper inside the
main application, reruns the Mac archive, executes Safari WebDriver smoke
coverage, then uploads that one ZIP under the exact package-darwin-arm64 name.

The Release workflow is triggered only by quarterly vYY.Q.PATCH tags. The
release tag therefore runs the complete reusable CI chain first; a standalone
ci-* tag runs CI only. Publication has repository write permission only in its
final job and never downloads or publishes Linux artifacts.

## Evidence

Portable distribution coverage verifies target admission, archive content,
version metadata, extension compilation, icons, release-tag rules, workflow
ordering, exact action major versions, Linux exclusion from Release, and the
single ARM64 release archive with its embedded Safari companion.

The Linux development archive has repeatedly passed assembly and extracted
package verification. The current workflow structure test proves general
validation precedes macos-arm, Release calls the reusable CI workflow, and the
publish job contains no npm/package command.

Current GitHub Actions and Node documentation support the pinned action majors,
pnpm cache use through setup-node, the ARM64 macOS runner, and direct execution
of erasable TypeScript under Node 24. Platform-specific Apple trust and native
acceptance are intentionally owned by task blooket-15.

On 2026-10-08, extracted-package verification was corrected to return the
current `{items, completeness}` bridge contract for synthetic My Sets reads.
This retains the explicit unknown collection completeness instead of
fabricating an exhausted list or using the rejected legacy bare-array format.

Package commands now accept a bounded `--output <name>` inside the existing
repository distribution directory. Separate acceptance builds reuse shared
dependencies/downloads without deleting or replacing an earlier package.
Assembly claims its output exclusively, rejects existing directories/archives,
and publishes a fully written/synced archive with a no-clobber hard link.

Portable regressions exercise the actual package CLI's malformed/duplicate
options and refusal of an existing archive before build activity. Synthetic
previous bytes remain unchanged. Names cannot select arbitrary paths; an
actual extracted package remains the integration gate for launcher/CLI behavior.

The first isolated assembly at commit `64fc808` failed when tar observed the
new partial archive changing its input directory during traversal. No package
was accepted; cleanup removed the owned output and left prior artifacts intact.
The correction reserves the excluded partial before traversal, with a real tar
regression verifying contents, absence of self-inclusion, and no-clobber retry.

The next full assembly exposed a shadowed archive variable: publication targeted
the existing cached Node archive and safely stopped with `EEXIST`. The final
distribution destination now has a distinct name; the guard preserved the cache
and owned failed output was removed. Neither failed run is package acceptance.

## Blockers

None. Signing, notarization, Gatekeeper, installed Safari behavior, native
Keychain/login-item behavior, and other platform-specific acceptance belong only
to task blooket-15.


On 2026-10-08, the clean committed `16c14dc` passed actual Linux assembly and
extracted-package release verification using the isolated
`acceptance-final-package` output namespace. This exercised the packaged
launcher,
single instance, media processing, canonical CLI, synthetic bridge reads, CSRF,
and owned shutdown. The session-owned 415 MiB assembly/archive was removed after
verification; pre-existing packages and the shared 230 MiB runtime cache were
preserved. Nonfatal postject ELF section warnings did not fail this acceptance.
